  "Name": "Dobrowolski1970-filterE-xbar",
  "Source": "J. A. Dobrowolski, \"Optical Interference Filters for the Adjustment of Spectral Response and Spectral Power Distribution,\" Appl. Opt. 9, 1396-1402 (1970). doi:10.1364/AO.9.001396 - Table II (Filter E), p. 1401; Sec. III.E, Fig. 10",
  "Notes": "x-bar tristimulus filter (CIE standard observer colour-matching function) for use with a Gillod-Boutry photocell, in series with absorbing glass cutoff filters (Sec. III.E)\nSpecification: Relative transmittance of the interference filter x glass filters (7.5 mm FG 10 and 0.92 mm BG 38, as labelled in Fig. 10(a); glass data not given) x photocell sensitivity (Fig. 9) should match the CIE 1931 x-bar colour-matching function (solid target curve of Fig. 10(a)); only the relative shape was specified, any peak transmittance > 50 % acceptable.\nReported: Peak transmittance of the x-bar filter combination: calculated 68 % (allowing for two air-glass surfaces), experimental 58 %, 47 % with additional correction filters (text p. 1401-1402, Fig. 10). These include the glass filters, so they are not checks of the stack alone. The computed transmittance of the 13-layer interference filter alone (dashed, Fig. 10(a)) follows both the blue (~0.44 um, ~0.25) and red (~0.6 um, ~1.0) lobes of the target with ~0.53 transmission spike near 0.39 um (plot readings). Fig. 10(b): calculated (target - normalized transmittance) of the cutoff + interference filter combination; worst case among the three filters (x-bar) is < +/-5 % of peak transmittance.\nNotes: Medium and substrate both 1.518 as printed (filter cemented between glass); the 'two air-glass surfaces' in the reported peak transmittances are the outer faces of the assembly. Identification: the text lists the 13-, 17- and 18-layer filters as E, F, G and Fig. 10 (x-bar) is labelled 13 LAYER FILTER, so E = x-bar. Thick layers 2, 3 and 12 (0.9141, 0.8738, 0.5822 um optical) are as printed. Table lists Substrate, layers 1..N, Medium; layer 1 is next to the substrate (the synthesis program grows the design from the bare substrate), so the order is REVERSED here (layer 1 below = last printed layer, next to the medium). Thicknesses are printed as optical thicknesses n*d in micrometres; they are entered as FWOT at reference_nm = 1000 nm, which is the same number (1 FWOT at 1000 nm = 1 um optical thickness), with constant, non-dispersive, lossless indices exactly as printed. Physical thickness d = OT/n. Normal incidence assumed (not stated). 13 layers, as stated in the text; summed optical thickness of the printed layers = 3.9068 um (no total is printed).\nLayers from Table II (Filter E), p. 1401; Sec. III.E, Fig. 10; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
